    "result": "Perfect! All tests are passing. Here's a summary of the fixes I made:\n\n## Summary of Fixes\n\nI fixed 2 failing tests in the frai-core test suite:\n\n### 1. **governance.test.js - Missing named exports** \n**Problem**: The test was trying to import `APPROVAL_STATUSES`, `DATA_SENSITIVITY_LEVELS`, `GOVERNANCE_SECTIONS`, and `RISK_LEVELS` as named exports from `./index.js`, but they were only exported in the default export object.\n\n**Fix**: Added named exports to `/packages/frai-core/src/finetune/index.js`:\n```javascript\nexport { APPROVAL_STATUSES, DATA_SENSITIVITY_LEVELS, GOVERNANCE_SECTIONS, RISK_LEVELS };\n```\n\n### 2. **eval/index.test.js - Wrong toxicityMetric signature**\n**Problem**: The `toxicityMetric` function had signature `(outputs, bannedTerms)`, but `runEvaluations` always calls metrics with `(outputs, references)`. When `references` were passed as `bannedTerms`, they were treated as banned words to check for, causing the toxicity score to be incorrect.\n\n**Fix**: Updated the `toxicityMetric` signature in `/packages/frai-core/src/eval/metrics.js` to:\n```javascript\nexport function toxicityMetric(outputs, references, bannedTerms = defaultBannedTerms)\n```\nNow it properly accepts both parameters and uses the actual banned terms list.\n\n**Results**: All 28 tests across 8 test files now pass \u2713",
